# {Project Name}

Kotlin Spring Boot backend with hexagonal architecture. Detekt enforces module boundaries.

## Scripts

```bash
./gradlew build      # Build all modules + Detekt
./gradlew bootRun    # Run application
./gradlew test       # Run all tests
./gradlew detekt     # Run Detekt only
```

## Architecture

```
├── domain/          # Business logic, entities, ports (no framework deps)
├── api/             # REST controllers, DTOs
├── adapters/        # Port implementations
└── app/             # Entry point, wires all modules
```

### Module Dependency Rules

| Module | Can Depend On | Cannot Depend On |
|--------|---------------|------------------|
| domain | (nothing) | api, adapters, app |
| api | domain | adapters, app |
| adapters/* | domain | api, other adapters, app |
| app | domain, api, adapters/* | (none - entry point) |

Detekt with hexagonal rules will catch violations at build time.

## Domain Layer

The domain module contains:
- **Entities** - Core business objects (immutable, no framework annotations)
- **Value Objects** - Typed wrappers using `@JvmInline value class`
- **Ports** - Interfaces defining required operations (suffix: Port, Repository, Gateway, Client)
- **Services** - Business logic orchestrating entities and ports

Example domain entity:

```kotlin
package {GROUP_ID}.domain.user

@JvmInline
value class UserId(val value: String)

data class User(
    val id: UserId,
    val name: String,
    val email: String
)
```

Example port:

```kotlin
package {GROUP_ID}.domain.user

interface UserRepository {
    suspend fun findById(id: UserId): User?
    suspend fun save(user: User): User
}
```

## API Layer

REST controllers that depend only on domain:

```kotlin
package {GROUP_ID}.api.user

import {GROUP_ID}.domain.user.UserRepository
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
class UserController(
    private val userRepository: UserRepository
) {
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    suspend fun getUser(@PathVariable id: String) =
        userRepository.findById(UserId(id))
}
```

## Adding an Adapter

1. Create `adapters/{name}/build.gradle.kts` with `implementation(project(":domain"))`
2. Add `include("adapters:{name}")` to settings.gradle.kts
3. Add `implementation(project(":adapters:{name}"))` to app/build.gradle.kts
4. Implement domain ports in the adapter

Example adapter:

```kotlin
package {GROUP_ID}.adapters.postgres

@Repository
class UserRepositoryAdapter(
    private val jpaRepository: UserJpaRepository
) : UserRepository {
    override suspend fun findById(id: UserId) =
        jpaRepository.findById(id.value)?.toDomain()
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Error Handling with Arrow

```kotlin
import arrow.core.Either
import arrow.core.left
import arrow.core.right

sealed class UserError {
    data object NotFound : UserError()
    data class ValidationFailed(val message: String) : UserError()
}

suspend fun findUser(id: UserId): Either<UserError, User> =
    userRepository.findById(id)?.right()
        ?: UserError.NotFound.left()
```

### Testing

```kotlin
@Test
fun `should find user by id`() = runTest {
    val user = User(UserId("1"), "Test", "test@example.com")
    val repository = InMemoryUserRepository(listOf(user))

    val result = repository.findById(UserId("1"))

    assertEquals(user, result)
}
```
